all over santiago long lines lead up to  new mobile vaccination centers to speed  up inoculations especially for people  under 30 who are now eligible but the  motivation for many of those getting  their jabs is not just fear of infection  health is important but i also want to  get the mobile path because they say it  will let us work and move around freely  despite the pandemic  the mobile pass or green passport was  implemented this week to allow people  who've been fully vaccinated to skip  many of the restrictions that apply to  everyone else they include going out  whenever you want and traveling to other  regions without a police permit which  might explain why santiago's bus  terminal is packed  in poland they're raffling cars to  reward those who are fully vaccinated  here in chile it's this mobility pass  that's the incentive but the problem is  that the sinovac chinese vaccine which  is what's most used in this country is  turning out not to be the silver bullet  that many had hoped  as in israel more than 50 percent of  chileans have been fully inoculated but  unlike in israel infections here  continue escalating up sixty percent in  the last two weeks here in the capital  the difference say epidemiologists is  the type of vaccine that's being used in  each country  vaccine is not very effective in  eliminating the circulation of the virus  it reduces symptomatic contagion by 50  to 67 percent as opposed to for example  the fisa vaccine that has around 95  percent efficacy in preventing contagion  the sinovac vaccine is extremely  effective in preventing serious cases of  coronavirus which in turn saves lives  there's also consensus that any vaccine  is better than none  and the chilean government is the only  one in latin america that's been able to  acquire enough to inoculate the entire  population but allowing people to move  around more freely with the virus still  circulating out of control is premature  says the doctor's guild  the government must